{"title":"VII.2 Shannon Entropy","markdown":{"headingText":"VII.2 Shannon Entropy","containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\nSuppose we have a discrete random variable $X$ with possible outcomes $x_1,x_2,\\dots,x_n$ each with corresponding probabilities $P(x_1), P(x_2),\\dots,P(x_n)$. \n\nThe **Shannon entropy**[^sh] is defined as\n\n[^sh]: **Entropy (information theory)** available from  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Entropy_(information_theory)\n\n$$H(x):=-\\sum_{i=1}^nP(x_i)\\log P(x_i),$$\n\nwhere $P(x_i)$ represents the probability of each outcome $x_i$.\n\nFirst, commenting on the input:\n1. Shannon entropy only works on a probability distribution, so all the input must be non-negative and add up to 1.\n2. For sake of focus, we will assume that *this is already solved.* \n3. Otherwise, this seems to be a routine case of a \"normalization step\" where each user input is divided by their sum, which is a combination of a **sum** and a **division** of the form\n$$\nP(x_i)=\\frac{x_i}{\\sum_{j=1}^{n} x_j}.\n$$\n\nWe should then end up with a vector $x = (P(x_1), \\ldots, P(x_n))$.\n\nNow, we need the logarithms of the probabilities. These can also be done in various ways; when applicable, we black-box the details, deferring to previous sections:\n1. we can use a Taylor series approximation, centered necessarily away from $0$.\n2. we can have the user simply give the $\\log(x_i)$ as part of their input.\n\nIn any case, we should end up with a vector $l$ with each $l_i = \\log(P(x_i))$.\n\nThen it suffices to compute the element-wise product $x * l * (-1)$ to obtain a single vector, after which using a sum operation gives\n$$H(x):=-\\sum_{i=1}^nP(x_i)\\log P(x_i)$$\n(or more precisely, a vector where every element is $H(x)$).\n\nThe main difficulties while computing entropy arise in situations where $x_i = 0$. In this case, L'Hopitals rule dictates that the correct contribution of the $-P(x) \\log P(x) = 0 * (\\infty)$ should be $0$. We have two suggestions, and it is not clear to us which one is better (it is likely to be very implementation-dependent):\n1. \"iron out\" the eventual answer with multiplication by an $\\texttt{equal(x_i, 0)}$ vector at the very end (before the summation), which would ensure all elements coming from an $x_i$ very close to $0$ actually contributes $0$;\n2. interrupt the computation right before the $\\log()$ step by **adding** the $\\texttt{equal(x_i, 0)}$ vector, which would switch the $x_i$s close to $0$ to close to $1$ (therefore making the $\\log(x_i) \\approx 0$.\n\nIn production, any method would require extensive testing to prevent blowup.","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"echo":true,"output":true,"warning":true,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb":null,"enabled":null,"daemon":null,"daemon-restart":false,"debug":false,"ipynb-filters":[],"ipynb-shell-interactivity":null,"plotly-connected":true,"engine":"markdown"},"render":{"keep-tex":false,"keep-typ":false,"keep-source":false,"keep-hidden":false,"prefer-html":false,"output-divs":true,"output-ext":"html","fig-align":"default","fig-pos":null,"fig-env":null,"code-fold":"none","code-overflow":"scroll","code-link":false,"code-line-numbers":false,"code-tools":false,"tbl-colwidths":"auto","merge-includes":true,"inline-includes":false,"preserve-yaml":false,"latex-auto-mk":true,"latex-auto-install":true,"latex-clean":true,"latex-min-runs":1,"latex-max-runs":10,"latex-makeindex":"makeindex","latex-makeindex-opts":[],"latex-tlmgr-opts":[],"latex-input-paths":[],"latex-output-dir":null,"link-external-icon":false,"link-external-newwindow":false,"self-contained-math":false,"format-resources":[],"notebook-links":true},"pandoc":{"standalone":true,"wrap":"none","default-image-extension":"png","to":"html","